  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1232 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nMid-week through late week undergoes a warming trend as ridging \nover the central CONUS and accompanying large scale sinking and \ndry motion strengthens. This allows highs to rise from the \n95F-100F range, 100F-105F along the Rio Grande and 85F-90F higher \nelevations, to 100F-105F, 90F-95F higher elevations, and 105F-110F\nfor the Big Bend. As a result, heat stress will be increasing, \nand whether or not heat advisory criteria is met anywhere, \neveryone is encouraged to take proper heat safety precautions \n(limit time spent outdoors during the hottest part of the day, \ntake frequent breaks in the shade if outdoors, drink plenty of \nwater, and do not leave people or pets unattended in locked \nvehicles). Lows however will not warm as much, while PoPs remain \nlow (less than 25%), higher over the northern parts of the \nforecast area farther away from the core of ridging over SW TX. No\nmajor flash flooding or severe weather is indicated at this time.\n\nThe late season hot temperatures resembling early August more \nthan mid September will be short-lived. NBM highs and lows CONUS \nwide along with WPC surface analyses indicate a slight cooling \ntrend late this weekend through early next week in temperatures \nrippling down from the Northern Great Plains, courtesy of weak \nwest-east oriented cold fronts from the Northern through Southern \nGreat Plains. While magnitude of cooling will be on the order of \nmore than 20F farther north across the Northern Great Plains where\nthe ridge will break down and give way to a more zonal mid to \nupper air pattern, this cooling will also bump highs down across \nthe area (but by less as mid to upper ridging holds for longer \nover the Southern Great Plains) into the 80F-95F range from the \n90F-105F range, and lows from the 65F-75F range into the 60F-70F \nrange. These series of weak cold fronts may also increase PoPs \ninto the 25% to 35% range, especially across the SE NM plains \nthrough northern Permian Basin. However, this far out, any further\nspecific details regarding timing, magnitude, extent, and \npotential for any severe weather remain hazy. We will continue to \nmonitor the upcoming weak cooldown and increased rain chances as \nwe draw closer to next week.",
